BUILDING MATERIALS ESTIMATOR

Building Materials Estimator — a real-world task using only today's concepts: importing and using the math module - math.sqrt() for square roots and math.ceil() for rounding UP - plus functions, parameters, return, while validation, comparisons, if / else, variables, input(), float(), arithmetic, and printing. No imports except math, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A young engineer quoting a small fence job in Port Harcourt needs three figures for any rectangular plot: the corner-to-corner diagonal, how many fence posts the perimeter needs when posts stand every 3 metres, and how many trips the materials tipper makes when it carries 7 cubic metres at a time. The catch: you cannot buy a third of a post or make half a trip - everything rounds UP, and that is exactly what math.ceil() is for.

The program must:
- Import math and use math.sqrt() and math.ceil() - both must appear.
- Ask for the plot width and length in metres (decimals allowed, at least 1).
- Diagonal = square root of (width squared + length squared). Perimeter = 2 x (width + length).
- A volume of waste soil is also given in cubic metres; trips = volume rounded UP to whole tipper loads of 7.
- Print:
  PLOT: 30.0m x 20.0m
  DIAGONAL: 36.06m
  PERIMETER: 100.0m
  POSTS NEEDED: 34
  TIPPER TRIPS: 5

Sample run:

Plot width (m): 30
Plot length (m): 20
Waste soil (cubic metres): 30

PLOT: 30.0m x 20.0m
DIAGONAL: 36.06m
PERIMETER: 100.0m
POSTS NEEDED: 34
TIPPER TRIPS: 5

Edge cases to handle: a perimeter that divides evenly by 3 (like 30m - exactly 10 posts, and ceil must NOT add one), volume exactly 7 (one trip), volume of 1 (still one trip - never zero), a square plot (width equals length), and decimals everywhere - checking the diagonal really is the square root, not a guess.
